## Using charts within social media

In a social media post, such as one sharing a household's year of solar power, use larger, bolder shapes so people take in the message quickly. You can pick out one result with an additional colour.

The example shows the most-used automations in a month, as a post might share them. Notice that only the top automation uses the additional colour.

<%= colour_example('chart-govuk-social', layout: :chart, callout: false) %>

<h2 id="give-each-series-a-stable-colour">Give each device a stable colour</h2>

With the civica companion installed, each device on the Energy dashboard keeps the same colour, pattern and marker when you add another device, hide a series or reorder the list. For example, the dishwasher keeps its colour whether or not the oven is shown.

Energy roles have fixed colours:

| Energy role | Colour | Pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid import | Primary blue | Solid |
| Grid export | Blue shade 50% | Solid |
| Solar | Orange tint 25% | Solid |
| Gas | Primary magenta | Solid |
| Water | Teal tint 25% | Solid |
| Non-fossil energy | Teal tint 25% | Diagonal |
| Battery charging | Purple tint 25% | Solid |
| Battery discharge | Purple tint 25% | Reverse diagonal |
| Total consumption | Black (White in dark mode) | Solid, with a dashed line |

The 6 colours cannot tell more than 6 devices apart, so colours repeat and the pattern, line dash and marker do the rest. The companion adds them to the native legend and keeps its controls to show and hide each series. [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) explains what to do when a chart gets crowded, and the [24-device chart](../components/chart-examples.html) shows it.

This example shows the humidity in 4 rooms over a day, in light and dark mode. Notice that the rooms take `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-4` in order, that each room keeps its colour in both modes, and that its line style, marker and name tell it apart without colour.

<%= example('data-colour-categorical') %>

### For developers

Pass the device's stable entity ID or statistic ID to `chartSeriesStyle(key, {mode})` from `design-system/chart-colours.mjs`. It returns one of the 6 categorical colours with a fill pattern, line dash and marker. The same ID always gives the same style, after a reload, a reorder or a legend change, so you do not need to save a colour for each device. Keep the ID when the friendly name changes. A previous-period comparison, and the positive and negative halves of a power source, share their base ID. Several meters in one role, such as 2 gas meters, use keys like `gas:<statistic ID>`. They keep the role colour and each gets its own pattern.

To change a chart colour or an energy role, follow [Maintain the colour records](../community/index.html#maintain-colour-records).

<h2 id="bg-col-07">Choose colours from what the data means</h2>

<h3 id="choose-the-colour-mapping">Match the colours to what they show</h3>

| What the colour shows | Colours | What the chart also needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A source, such as a device | The [categorical palette](#categorical-palette) | A stable ID, a readable name and a pattern, line style or marker |
| An amount that goes up in one direction | The tints and shades of one [scale family](#sequential-and-divergent-scale-palette) | The measure, unit, lowest and highest values, and what happens outside them |
| A difference above or below a centre | Two scale families with a neutral centre | A centre that means something, such as a target temperature, and signed values |
| No reading | None | Words such as “Not supplied” and a mark that is not a number |

A categorical colour is not a rank or an amount: do not use the categorical colours to show temperatures rising. A real zero belongs on the scale. An unknown or unavailable reading does not.

### Define a scale before you choose colours

For a heatmap or a map layer that shows an amount, write down the quantity, unit, period, lowest and highest values and any centre first. Then use the listed steps of one family, from light to dark. If a value falls outside the scale, say so: giving it the end colour hides it. If the range changes, update the key and say whether charts from different periods can still be compared.

Keep labels outside coloured areas unless you have tested the text on that fill. Pale steps can disappear on a white background, so add outlines and direct labels. Give a missing value a crossed empty swatch and the words “Not supplied”, never a colour from the scale.

This example shows a week of energy used in each room on a 0 to 2 kWh scale, and a week of room temperatures as a difference from a 20 °C target, as 2 heatmaps. Notice that each cell prints its value, and that a missing reading is a crossed empty cell with no colour from the scale.

<%= example('data-colour-quantitative') %>

## In Home Assistant

The civica theme gives native charts these colours, but each card draws them in its own way:

- **Energy dashboard cards**: with the optional civica companion installed, each energy role and device gets the colour, pattern, line dash and marker on this page, and a **View data** table. Without the companion, energy roles keep their colours but devices take the numbered colours by position.
- **History graph and Statistics graph cards**: these colour each series by its position in the card, using the theme's numbered colours `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`, which the theme also sets as Home Assistant's `color-1` to `color-24`. They draw solid lines with no patterns, dashes or end labels, so give each entity a clear name. A series can change colour if you reorder the entities in the card.

The numbered colours follow the energy roles first, then repeat the 6 categorical colours. 24 is not a limit on the number of series:

| Numbered colour | Colour | Energy role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `civica-chart-1` | Primary blue | Grid import |
| `civica-chart-2` | Primary magenta | Gas |
| `civica-chart-3` | Teal tint 25% | Non-fossil energy |
| `civica-chart-4` | Blue shade 50% | Grid export |
| `civica-chart-5` | Orange tint 25% | Solar |
| `civica-chart-6` | Teal tint 25% | Water |
| `civica-chart-7` | Purple tint 25% | Battery charging |
| `civica-chart-8` | Purple tint 25% | Battery discharge |
| `civica-chart-9` | Black (White in dark mode) | Total consumption |
| `civica-chart-10` to `civica-chart-24` | Primary blue, Blue shade 50%, Primary magenta, Purple tint 25%, Teal tint 25%, Orange tint 25%, then again from the start | None |

[Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) owns the rest of the chart specification, such as line widths and the zero line, and [Chart examples](../components/chart-examples.html) has working Energy charts. [Data visualisation](brand-data.html) explains how to choose a chart.
